"explanation": "Soil formation (pedogenesis) requires the breakdown of parent material into smaller particles and the accumulation of organic matter. A rotting log undergoes rapid biological decomposition by fungi, bacteria, and insects, converting organic material into humus, which is a critical component of topsoil. This process occurs on a timescale of months to years. In contrast, the weathering of a rock (Option C) is a slow geological process taking centuries to millennia. Water in a stream (Option B) and waves on a beach (Option D) primarily transport and deposit existing sediments rather than creating new soil in place through weathering and organic accumulation. Therefore, the rotting log contributes to the formation of new soil the fastest.",
